From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 05:25:04 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: gate pending timeline waits before Metal
 submission

Use on-demand common submission for unsatisfied timeline waits so Metal cannot retire a native event wait before the host signals it. Move reusable binary payloads correctly and stop the worker before device resource destruction.

Answer VK_SYNC_WAIT_PENDING with whether a signal operation has been submitted rather than whether it completed. Track the highest value already signalled on the CPU or enqueued on the queue's Metal timeline, and treat a binary semaphore wait as pending because its signal must have been submitted first. Otherwise the common runtime reads every unfinished dependency as unsubmitted: it turns the assisted submission worker on for the life of the device and blocks the caller of vkQueueSubmit, which on a virtualized device is the thread that must deliver the release the worker is waiting for.

The queue publishes that value just before it enqueues the signal, not after. Every later submission to the device's one queue runs after this one, so nothing can order native work behind the value before the signal is in place, and nothing may touch the sync once the signal is enqueued: Metal can deliver it at once, and whoever sees it delivered may destroy the fence or semaphore straight away, as vkQueueWaitIdle does with its own.

In a threaded queue a binary payload moves when the worker reaches the wait, not inside vkQueueSubmit. The signal that payload waits for may still be queued ahead of the wait, and the worker unwraps it against whatever payload the semaphore holds when it runs, so a move made at once handed that signal the fresh payload and left the wait on a point nothing signals until Metal abandoned the native wait seconds later.

Keep absolute CPU wait deadlines while observing device loss, wake queue drains when submission fails, and allow lost-device cleanup of pending submissions. Cover delayed host signals, mixed binary waits with every signalled stage bounded to a second, loss during waits and drain, a binary wait queued behind a host-gated batch, and submission that outruns a long GPU dependency.

Once the worker runs, a resubmitted command buffer is recorded again on it, while the application may be recording another buffer from the same command pool: Vulkan asks it to synchronize the pool only against its own recording, and vkQueueSubmit is not one of those commands. Both recordings take upload BOs from the pool's free list and give them back, so the list and its count are now guarded by a pool mutex. kosmickrisp_command_pool_threads records from one pool on both threads with the worker forced on; without the mutex the list lost its count or crashed within a run.
